const e="armageddon-cosplay-contest",u=null,o="Read the whole 10-page rules PDF. It prints no fee, no marks or criteria weights, no time limits and no dates (closing dates are on the Expo website). It prints a prize list in dollars without naming a currency; it is kept as printed. Contact: only the shared event email is printed.",t=JSON.parse(`{"Title":"Rules & Criteria","Subtitle":"Armageddon Cosplay Contest (regional rounds). Read the rules before you register.","NoticeTitle":"Please Read Before Registering","Notice":"Your registration form is only a register of interest. Your spot is not secured until your construction diary (PDF) has been received. Registration closing dates: {{RegistrationDeadline}}.","Notes":["All prizes are subject to change.","You must be at the event for both your judging session and the Main Stage presentation."],"Sections":[{"Key":"general","Title":"General Rules","Items":["The cosplay contest is focused only on craft and construction.","The judge can move entrants between the divisions as they see fit.","Each regional event has a professional judge who works in the costuming industry outside the cosplay community. The organisers and judges follow the same rules.","Rules are changed to let the judges run the contest as fully as possible. Where possible, notice of a change is given."]},{"Key":"eligibility","Title":"Eligibility","Items":["One entry per person per contest.","You can enter each regional contest with a new costume. You cannot enter a costume you have already entered in a contest.","You cannot enter a costume, prop or skit that has already won an award in any other contest. If you won another contest, you can enter this contest in the same year with a different costume.","No professional costumiers can take part. Professional means a person who earns money from a creative skill that can be used in cosplay, for example makeup, film or clothing alterations.","If you have studied costume or a costume-related field for more than a year, enter the Open division.","If you have received a Novice Best Solo, a Novice Best Group or three Novice Technical Awards, you must enter the Open division.","If you have received any Open award, you cannot compete in Novice any more.","You must be the maker of at least 75% of your costume or costumes and props. If you received help, say so on the registration form.","Costumes are accepted from movies, TV, musicals, anime, cartoons, manga, comic books, games, card games, figurines and similar. For anything else, check with the judging team first.","Fan art is accepted only as a fan interpretation (an alternative costume) that you chose to cosplay. You must give reference images of the fan art design and of the official art. Without reference images, the entry is not accepted.","You must be present at the event for both your judging session and the Main Stage presentation."]},{"Key":"registration","Title":"How to Register","Items":["Submit the online registration form. Your costume does not need to be finished when you submit it.","Include as much of your construction diary as you can with the form. Send the diary as a PDF. If it is not in the right format or not labelled correctly, it is not accepted.","Your completed form is only a register of interest. Your entry is not complete, and your spot is not secured, until your construction diary has been received.","Send the construction diary before the registration closing date. Closing dates: {{RegistrationDeadline}}.","You do not get a confirmation email when you submit an entry. This is normal.","After the closing date, all applications are reviewed. You then get an acceptance or a decline email. An acceptance email gives your interview time and the judging room location.","These emails can take a number of days after entries close. Please be patient.","A declined entry means the rules in this document were not followed. The error is explained to you. If it is minor, you get a limited time to fix it ({{AmendmentWindow}}). Not sending the construction diary is not a minor error. After the fix, you get an email with your interview time.","Your construction diary is your chance to show all your work. Give as much detail about your costume as you can.","In the diary, show: reference images of the characters; how you made each costume and prop; the materials you chose and why; the techniques you used and why; and the components and how complex they are.","For sewn costumes, cover pattern making, fabric choice, sewing, tailoring, quality of finish, hems and seams, lining, buttons and embellishments, and accuracy.","For armour, cover materials, construction, techniques, durability, paint finish and accuracy.","For wigs, cover colour match, styling, setting, additions and accuracy.","For props, cover materials, design, size accuracy, safety, colour accuracy and finish.","For makeup and prosthetics, cover application, blending, durability and construction. For moulding and sculpting, cover the process, materials, accuracy and finish. Also describe trial and error: new techniques tested and problems solved.","Also answer these questions: Is there anything you are particularly proud of? Were there any surprises while making the items that made the work easier or harder? If your entry is unfinished, how do you plan to finish it (this is followed up at the interview)? Was any part of your costume not made by you, such as bought items or help received?","You can add any other information you think helps your entry."]},{"Key":"dates","Title":"Dates","Items":["Registration closing dates for the current round: {{RegistrationDeadline}}.","The Main Stage schedule is published {{SchedulePublication}} before the event."]},{"Key":"venue","Title":"Judging Room and Main Stage","Items":["The judging room location is emailed to you after applications close. If you do not know the venue, arrive early enough to find the room.","The public presentation is on the Main Stage at the time given in the Armageddon schedule."]},{"Key":"judging","Title":"Judging Process","Items":["Be outside the judging room {{JudgingArrival}}.","Do not be late. If you may be late, message the judging team right away. If you are late, wait outside the room and the team tries to fit you in without disturbing others.","The judge and Armageddon Expo staff are not responsible if you miss the interview for any reason. They cannot promise another time.","The interview is a short, friendly chat to follow up on what you wrote. You can point out the most impressive parts of your costume and anything missing or unfinished in your diary. You are not asked to answer in character.","Photographs are taken at the interview to help judging and to record your entry.","The interview only shows the outcome of your work. Your registration form and diary should hold all the information on how the elements were made.","After the interview you are free to enjoy the rest of the show.","When all contestants are seen, the doors close and judging begins. Photos, registrations and interview notes are collected and awards are decided. This can take several hours. Each element is considered.","Contestants who need help for any reason, including health or disability, can bring a helper to the interview. Tell the organisers about other help you need, for example being judged earlier or later in the day."]},{"Key":"levels","Title":"Regional Events and National Final","Items":["Awards are presented at each regional event at the discretion of the Armageddon Cosplay Judge.","All regional Best in Show winners are in the National Armageddon Cosplay Contest Final automatically. It is held at Auckland Armageddon (Spring).","Regional Best in Show winners are invited to represent themselves on stage at Auckland Armageddon for the announcement of the Armageddon Cosplay Contest Champion.","The Champion is chosen in Auckland on the Monday after the Auckland regional Main Stage presentation.","Best in Show winners from each city are encouraged to wear their winning cosplay and perform their skit at the Auckland Main Stage presentation. This is optional."]},{"Key":"prizes","Title":"Awards and Prizes","Items":["Best Youth (age 5 to 13 only): {{BestYouthPrize}}.","Novice Best Solo: {{BestSoloPrize}}.","Judge's Technical Award: {{TechnicalAwardPrize}}.","Runner Up Best in Show (regional winner): {{RunnerUpPrize}}.","Best in Show (regional winner): {{BestInShowPrize}}.","Armageddon Cosplay Contest Champion: {{ChampionPrize}}.","All prizes are subject to change."]},{"Key":"teams","Title":"Pairs and Groups","Items":["You can enter the right division as a pair or as part of a group.","In a group, all members must have contributed equally to the costumes and props. The average score of all members is the group score.","The work can be done by each person, or shared by experience, for example one member makes the props, one styles the wigs and one works on costume pieces."]},{"Key":"conduct","Title":"Conduct and Responsibility","Items":["Bullying of staff, other contestants or anyone else involved is not accepted.","Please be kind to other contestants, who also have an allotted time to be seen.","Only props, costumes and people shown in the judging are allowed on the Main Stage for the presentation.","Be at the Main Stage {{StageArrival}} so that technical checks, props and other preparation are complete.","If any member of a group drops out, the remaining members can still be judged and go on stage.","The Armageddon Cosplay organisers are not responsible for lost forms, or for damage to you or your costume, or damage caused by you or your costume.","The event is billed as family friendly but has a varied audience. Choose a costume you feel comfortable wearing in a large and diverse crowd. No costume is no costume.","Choose body makeup or paint carefully, thinking of your health and safety and the clothes and costumes of other people at the Expo."]},{"Key":"complaints","Title":"Declined Entries and Questions","Items":["If your registration is declined, the reason is explained. Everyone must follow these rules and exceptions cannot be made. After the explanation there is no more correspondence.","Send questions to {{OrganiserContact}}. More information and FAQs are on {{ExpoWebsite}}."]}],"Documents":[{"Title":"Cosplay Competition Rules (PDF)","Url":"https://armageddonexpo.com/resources/files/picker/armageddon-cosplay-competition-rules.pdf"},{"Title":"Armageddon Expo website","Url":"http://www.armageddonexpo.com/"}]}`),n=[{ParticipantType:"Team",Source:"Cosplay Competition Rules p.3 (Pairs or Groups)",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["A Novice group must have only Novice contestants. If any one member is above Novice level, the whole group must enter Open.","All members must have contributed equally to the costumes and props. The average score of all members is the group score."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- A Novice group must have only Novice contestants. If any one member is above Novice level, the whole group must enter Open.
- All members must have contributed equally to the costumes and props. The average score of all members is the group score.`}],a=[{AgeGroup:"Junior",Source:"Cosplay Competition Rules p.2-3 (Youth, Junior) and p.9",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["For children 5 to 13 years old only.","Parents can help for up to 50%.","Maximum five entries per city.","A parent or guardian must come with the contestant to the interview and the performance, and must be named on the registration form. They can form a group with the child or be there only as a minder."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- For children 5 to 13 years old only.
- Parents can help for up to 50%.
- Maximum five entries per city.
- A parent or guardian must come with the contestant to the interview and the performance, and must be named on the registration form. They can form a group with the child or be there only as a minder.`},{AgeGroup:"Novice",Source:"Cosplay Competition Rules p.3 (Novice)",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["For beginners at costume making. Think about your real level of experience with the techniques you use. They may not be defined as cosplay. Please be fair to people who are really new to any part of this hobby.","The Novice Best Solo award is for this division."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:["The rules print no age for Novice and Open. The template uses ages 14 to 99 so they do not share ages with Junior."]},Markdown:`## Rules
- For beginners at costume making. Think about your real level of experience with the techniques you use. They may not be defined as cosplay. Please be fair to people who are really new to any part of this hobby.
- The Novice Best Solo award is for this division.

## Notes for participants
- The rules print no age for Novice and Open. The template uses ages 14 to 99 so they do not share ages with Junior.`},{AgeGroup:"Open",Source:"Cosplay Competition Rules p.3 (Open)",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Open to all non-professionals.","If you are new to the contest, you are welcome to enter Open if you choose."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- Open to all non-professionals.
- If you are new to the contest, you are welcome to enter Open if you choose.`},{AgeGroup:"Novice Pairs and Groups",Source:"Cosplay Competition Rules p.3 (Pairs or Groups)",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["A Novice group must have only Novice contestants. If any one member is above Novice level, the whole group must enter Open."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- A Novice group must have only Novice contestants. If any one member is above Novice level, the whole group must enter Open.`},{AgeGroup:"Open Pairs and Groups",Source:"Cosplay Competition Rules p.3 (Pairs or Groups)",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["A group with any member above Novice level enters Open."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- A group with any member above Novice level enters Open.`}],s=[{Title:"Cosplay Contest",Source:"Cosplay Competition Rules p.4-6 (Costumes and Props Guidelines)",Sections:{About:["A costume contest judged on craft and construction, by interview and by the construction diary."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["You must be the maker of at least 75% of your costume and props.","The 25% leeway allows buying items that cannot be made, copied or replaced in a practical way, for example shoes, wigs, bought clothing items or props.","All bought clothing items must be changed or be only a minor part of the costume, even for simple costumes. Their origin should not be recognisable.","Bought items include regular clothing and costumes, whether second hand, off the shelf, from your own wardrobe or commissioned.","Real life and imitation military clothing is not allowed. One general camouflage-patterned item, such as trousers or a top by itself, is allowed. A full camouflage outfit or an imitation military uniform is not allowed. This includes SWAT style uniforms. Military uniform from before 1900 is allowed.","Prop weapons are inoperable weapons that support the look of your costume or character. A prop weapon cannot be more than 2 metres long or weigh more than 5 kilograms. It should be made of a material suited to a convention, such as foam, plastic, rubber, lightweight wood or fabric.","Oversized or heavy costumes and props are a hazard and often cannot be shown on stage. If you plan one, email the organisers first. Think about moving through crowds, steps and doorways.","Loose metal chain accessories must be less than 30 centimetres long.","All prop bows must be unstrung or have a low-tensile thread. Prop arrows must have non-metal, blunted tips."],Allowed:["Metal armour is allowed when it is fixed to a costume and has no exposed sharp edges, for example chainmail, bolts and hinges, small screws and nails, jewellery, goblets and cups.","Lightsabers with a metal hilt are allowed at Armageddon if the whole item weighs less than 1 kg."],NotAllowed:["Real weapons: they cannot be entered or brought into Armageddon Expo, with no exceptions.","Firearms of any kind, including rifles, shotguns, hand guns, blowguns, airsoft guns, BB guns, paintball guns and sports weapons. Imitation firearms.","Tasers and laser devices, for example laser pointers. Explosives or other incendiary devices.","Metal weapons, for example knives, swords, daggers, switchblades, axes, hatchets, metal pole-arms and ninja stars.","Blunt weapons and sports equipment, for example nunchucks, brass knuckles, whips, baseball bats, clubs, paddles, poles and staffs.","Household items and tools or power tools, for example chainsaws, axes, drills and kitchen utensils.","Homemade stilts. Roller skates, skateboards, hoverboards, heelys and scooters. Drones or other motorised or remote controlled items.","Helium balloons. E-cigarettes or other vaping devices. Lighters, matches or other flammable sources."],Judging:["Judged by one professional costuming judge. Each element is considered, using the registration form, the construction diary, the interview and the photographs."],Points:null,Notes:["Registration closing dates are on the Armageddon Expo website."]},Markdown:`## About this competition
- A costume contest judged on craft and construction, by interview and by the construction diary.

## Rules
- You must be the maker of at least 75% of your costume and props.
- The 25% leeway allows buying items that cannot be made, copied or replaced in a practical way, for example shoes, wigs, bought clothing items or props.
- All bought clothing items must be changed or be only a minor part of the costume, even for simple costumes. Their origin should not be recognisable.
- Bought items include regular clothing and costumes, whether second hand, off the shelf, from your own wardrobe or commissioned.
- Real life and imitation military clothing is not allowed. One general camouflage-patterned item, such as trousers or a top by itself, is allowed. A full camouflage outfit or an imitation military uniform is not allowed. This includes SWAT style uniforms. Military uniform from before 1900 is allowed.
- Prop weapons are inoperable weapons that support the look of your costume or character. A prop weapon cannot be more than 2 metres long or weigh more than 5 kilograms. It should be made of a material suited to a convention, such as foam, plastic, rubber, lightweight wood or fabric.
- Oversized or heavy costumes and props are a hazard and often cannot be shown on stage. If you plan one, email the organisers first. Think about moving through crowds, steps and doorways.
- Loose metal chain accessories must be less than 30 centimetres long.
- All prop bows must be unstrung or have a low-tensile thread. Prop arrows must have non-metal, blunted tips.

## Allowed
- Metal armour is allowed when it is fixed to a costume and has no exposed sharp edges, for example chainmail, bolts and hinges, small screws and nails, jewellery, goblets and cups.
- Lightsabers with a metal hilt are allowed at Armageddon if the whole item weighs less than 1 kg.

## Not allowed
- Real weapons: they cannot be entered or brought into Armageddon Expo, with no exceptions.
- Firearms of any kind, including rifles, shotguns, hand guns, blowguns, airsoft guns, BB guns, paintball guns and sports weapons. Imitation firearms.
- Tasers and laser devices, for example laser pointers. Explosives or other incendiary devices.
- Metal weapons, for example knives, swords, daggers, switchblades, axes, hatchets, metal pole-arms and ninja stars.
- Blunt weapons and sports equipment, for example nunchucks, brass knuckles, whips, baseball bats, clubs, paddles, poles and staffs.
- Household items and tools or power tools, for example chainsaws, axes, drills and kitchen utensils.
- Homemade stilts. Roller skates, skateboards, hoverboards, heelys and scooters. Drones or other motorised or remote controlled items.
- Helium balloons. E-cigarettes or other vaping devices. Lighters, matches or other flammable sources.

## Judging
- Judged by one professional costuming judge. Each element is considered, using the registration form, the construction diary, the interview and the photographs.

## Notes for participants
- Registration closing dates are on the Armageddon Expo website.`}],i=[],r=[{Key:"RegistrationDeadline",Kind:"text",Label:"Registration closing date for the current round",Value:"See the Armageddon Expo website for the closing dates of the current round of regional competitions",Source:"Cosplay Competition Rules p.6 (Registration Closing Dates)"},{Key:"SchedulePublication",Kind:"text",Label:"When the Main Stage schedule is published",Value:"2 to 3 weeks before the event",Source:"Cosplay Competition Rules p.9 (The Judging Process)"},{Key:"JudgingArrival",Kind:"time",Label:"Arrive outside the judging room before your interview",Value:"At least 10 minutes before the interview time",Source:"Cosplay Competition Rules p.8 (Judging)"},{Key:"StageArrival",Kind:"time",Label:"Arrive before the Main Stage presentation",Value:"15 minutes before the presentation time",Source:"Cosplay Competition Rules p.9 (The Judging Process)"},{Key:"AmendmentWindow",Kind:"text",Label:"Time allowed to fix a minor registration error",Value:"Generally 24 hours, depending on the correction",Source:"Cosplay Competition Rules p.6 (Email Confirmation)"},{Key:"BestYouthPrize",Kind:"prize",Label:"Best Youth award (Junior, 5-13 only)",Value:"Certificate + $50",Source:"Cosplay Competition Rules p.2 (Awards and Prizes)"},{Key:"BestSoloPrize",Kind:"prize",Label:"Novice Best Solo award",Value:"Certificate + $250",Source:"Cosplay Competition Rules p.2 (Awards and Prizes)"},{Key:"TechnicalAwardPrize",Kind:"prize",Label:"Judge's Technical Award",Value:"Certificate + $50",Source:"Cosplay Competition Rules p.2 (Awards and Prizes)"},{Key:"RunnerUpPrize",Kind:"prize",Label:"Runner Up Best in Show (regional)",Value:"Certificate + $300",Source:"Cosplay Competition Rules p.2 (Awards and Prizes)"},{Key:"BestInShowPrize",Kind:"prize",Label:"Best in Show (regional winner)",Value:"Certificate + $500 + Auckland Armageddon Weekend Pass or Passes",Source:"Cosplay Competition Rules p.2 (Awards and Prizes)"},{Key:"ChampionPrize",Kind:"prize",Label:"Armageddon Cosplay Contest Champion",Value:"Certificate + $2000",Source:"Cosplay Competition Rules p.2 (Awards and Prizes)"},{Key:"OrganiserContact",Kind:"contact",Label:"Questions to the organisers",Value:{Name:"Armageddon Cosplay organisers",Role:"Questions and queries",Phone:"",WhatsApp:"",Email:"cosplay@armageddonexpo.com"},Source:"Cosplay Competition Rules p.10 (Disclaimer)"},{Key:"ExpoWebsite",Kind:"link",Label:"Armageddon Expo website (FAQs and closing dates)",Value:"http://www.armageddonexpo.com/",Source:"Cosplay Competition Rules p.10 (Disclaimer)"},{Key:"Announcements",Kind:"news",Label:"Notices printed in the rules",Value:[{Date:"",Title:"Prizes and rules can change",Text:"All prizes are subject to change. The rules and prizes are subject to change, and where possible notice of changes is given.",Url:""},{Date:"",Title:"Online entries only",Text:"All entries are submitted online with a digital form.",Url:""}],Source:"Cosplay Competition Rules p.2, p.6, p.10"}],l=[{Claim:"Armageddon Expo (New Zealand) cosplay contest rules: divisions, eligibility, costume and prop rules, registration with construction diary, interview judging, awards and prizes, regional events and the national final.",SourceType:"Primary - the event's own rules document",Source:"Armageddon Cosplay Competition Rules (10 pages)",Url:"https://armageddonexpo.com/resources/files/picker/armageddon-cosplay-competition-rules.pdf"}],d={TemplateKey:e,Year:null,Note:o,Page:t,ParticipantTypes:n,AgeGroups:a,Competitions:s,CompetitionAgeGroups:i,Variables:r,Evidence:l};export{a as AgeGroups,i as CompetitionAgeGroups,s as Competitions,l as Evidence,o as Note,t as Page,n as ParticipantTypes,e as TemplateKey,r as Variables,u as Year,d as default};
